# Build context for `docker build -f packages/studio/Dockerfile .`
#
# The context is the REPO ROOT (the Dockerfile explains why: two packages plus
# `assets/`), so without this the daemon would tar a 700MB tree — most of it
# node_modules and .next — before the first instruction runs.
#
# Everything here is either reinstalled in the image, rebuilt in the image, or
# must NOT be in the image at all. The third category is the one that matters.

# --- reinstalled / rebuilt --------------------------------------------------
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/*.tsbuildinfo
peeled/
bare-core/
pre-build/

# --- MUST NOT be in the image ----------------------------------------------
# The persisted volume (§2.2). An image carrying `data/` looks correct on its
# first boot and silently discards every write on the next redeploy, because the
# baked copy is restored underneath the mount point. Seeding production is a
# deliberate one-shot step against the VOLUME (`deploy/seed-data.sh`), never a
# COPY. This line is load-bearing.
packages/studio/data/

# Credentials. `.env` holds FIRECRAWL_API_KEY and anything else key-shaped, and
# `.auth-secret` is the HMAC the session cookies are signed with — a secret that
# reached an image layer has to be rotated, not deleted.
**/.env
**/.env.local
**/.auth-secret

# --- working exhaust, not source -------------------------------------------
.git/
.git*
.history/
.firecrawl/
.claude/
logs/
build/archive/
assets/archive/
**/*.bmpx
**/*.swbak

# --- the local-only halves of the app --------------------------------------
# The Build Manager shells out to a build tree that exists on one workstation.
# In a container it finds nothing and lists nothing, which is correct; shipping
# its inputs would be shipping a build system to a web host.
build/projects/
